<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Andechs</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Andechs-Merania, Counts of Andechs, Andechs-Meranien, counts of Andechs</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The counts of Andechs were a Bavarian noble family named after their castle above the Ammersee. In 1180 they took the title of dukes of Merania, and they held wide lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a>, Istria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a>. Their daughters included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hedwig-of-silesia">Saint Hedwig of Silesia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agnes-of-merania">Agnes of Merania</a>, wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france">Philip II of France</a>. The male line died out in 1248, and Andechs later became a monastery.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-andechs.jpg" alt="House of Andechs" width="320" height="483" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andechs-Wappen.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
